@nsobid-0EFFE4AF1DEC3A7966B934D4A7C3D2BF566A9C62

# Paper Mario: The Thousand-Year Door 1.0.1 - 60 FPS

@flag print_values
@flag offset_shift 0x100

// Runs the game at 60 FPS without the romfs edit the original 60 FPS mod
// needed. Replaces [60FPS v1.0.1] and [Faster Text Speed v1.0.1].
//
// The frame timer measures the step as the number of 60 Hz vsyncs since the
// last frame, clamped to at least 2. Presenting every vsync and clamping to 1
// gives a step of 1 at 60 FPS. Almost every system already scales by this
// step, including scripts, whose waits and loops count 60 Hz frames. The
// original mod also halved the millisecond step of the scripted MovePos
// command, which made every such move take twice as long; that is dropped.
//
// Message text prints a budget of characters per processed update, charged
// 2 / step per character, then waits the text speed (24 ms by default) before
// the next update. At 30 FPS the wait never blocks and each 33 ms update
// prints the budget. At 60 FPS the wait blocks every other update and each
// character costs twice as much, so text ran at half speed. Text now waits at
// least 33 ms between processed updates and charges one unit per character,
// which reproduces the 30 FPS cadence and keeps longer waits in milliseconds.
//
// Mash action commands (Run Away, Goombella's Rally Wink, Yoshi's Ground
// Pound and Stampede, Koops' Shell Slam, Ms. Mowz's Love Slap, Tease and
// Smooch, Bobbery's Bob-ombast) fill their gauge in 1/60 s sub-steps and add
// the press gain in every sub-step of the update that saw the press. A press
// was worth two sub-steps at 30 FPS and one at 60 FPS while the drain stayed
// time-based. The gain is now scaled by 2 / vsync count, so a press adds the
// stock amount at any frame rate. The original mod lowered these moves'
// difficulty in data_battle_weapon_party instead.
//
// Punis move by velocity x min(step, 2), and the mover damps velocity by 0.6
// per update, so the per-update acceleration reaches the stock top speed at
// any frame rate. The follow state instead tested per-update movement, which
// halves at 60 FPS. A puni starting from rest moves 0.1 x an approximate unit
// direction, which sat on the 0.1 stop limit and stopped it before it moved;
// moving punis also dropped to their idle state, and the give-up timer ran
// whenever a puni closed on its leader by less than 0.01 per update. The stop
// test now reads the velocity (below 0.05), and the moving and progress
// thresholds are scaled by min(step, 2), which matches 30 FPS.
//
// Other puni states count updates. The group's frame counter gains 1.0 per
// update and drives sprite animation frames, bob phases and every-fourth-
// frame scheduling; it now gains min(step, 2) / 2. The jump and fall states
// move by velocity and add -0.045 gravity once per update, and the slide
// state moves by its speed once per update; both now scale by
// min(step, 2) / 2. Puni sprites turn a quarter of the way toward the camera
// each update; at 60 FPS they turn 1 - sqrt(0.75) per update, which gives the
// same turn per 1/30 s.
//
// Characters turn by easing toward the new facing, 0.3 (or 0.5) of the
// remaining angle per 30 Hz tick. The turn update runs every frame but its
// tick flag is only set on the update where the step accumulator reaches 2,
// so at 60 FPS turns moved every other frame. The flag now carries the
// elapsed time in ticks and each turn moves 1 - (1 - k)^ticks of the way,
// which matches the 30 FPS turn speed at any frame rate.
//
// Moving floors add their turn speed to their angle once per script update,
// so a turning floor would spin twice as fast. The speed is scaled by
// min(step, 2) / 2. No shipped map sets up a turning floor.
//
// The Paper Game runners run native AI that counts updates rather than time
// (gravity, fan push and animation blends per update), so it runs as a fixed
// 30 Hz tick: the game step accumulates, and each time it reaches 2 the
// update runs with every step value set to 2.
// Each runner is then shown between its last two computed positions by the
// leftover fraction, one tick behind, so runners move smoothly and late
// frames do not hitch. A runner is only moved while it still shows the last
// blended position, so resets and scripted moves are left alone, and jumps of
// more than 20 units snap instead of blending. The blend state lives in the
// unused tail of the last .bss page.
//
// Guard, Superguard, timed press, Stylish and button prompt windows count
// 60 Hz frames and need no change. At 30 FPS every window rounded up to whole
// updates, so odd windows are a frame shorter at 60 FPS, as on GameCube.
//
// Shell Shield slows its swing while the stick is held by a per-update amount
// truncated to whole units: -2 per 60 Hz update at full tilt but -5 per 30 Hz
// update, so the shell swung twice as fast. The 30 Hz amount is now truncated
// and split over two updates.
//
// The added code sits in three dead static initialisers with no code or data
// references, and in repeated static-init blocks of sub_71004780D0 that never
// run because an earlier check of the same guard dominates them.
@enabled
// Frame rate: present every vsync, step of 1 vsync
00204740 35008052   // MOV W21, #0x1 = swap interval 1 (was MOV W21, W2)
002E77A4 29008052   // MOV W9, #0x1 = minimum vsync count 1 (was MOV W9, #2)
002E77CC 1F050071   // CMP W8, #0x1 = minimum vsync count 1 (was CMP W8, #2)
